[{"expectations":"Every letter is a real letter of the script, its romanization follows the standard system, its sound cue is right for the learner's language (Japanese vowels short and pure, Korean plain stops unaspirated, Russian false friends named), audioText is what a voice should say for it, and forms are only real positional forms (Arabic), never invented. A wrong sound, a wrong romanization or an invented form teaches the learner to read the letter wrong.","id":"accuracy","label":"Accurate letters and sounds"},{"expectations":"A good first lesson: the smallest useful set to read something real in the order a teacher presents it (hiragana vowels and K, Hangul basics that build blocks, Cyrillic look-alikes that sound different, Arabic letters with their joining), an intro with the one practical idea needed to read them (not history), a clear can-do and a short summary. Notice a lesson that dumps many letters at once, which a learner can't hold after one lesson, or an intro that doesn't help them read.","id":"pedagogy","label":"Right first letters, useful intro"},{"expectations":"Everything the learner reads is in their language and its variant (US English, Brazilian Portuguese), except the script and romanization; characters in the intro carry their romanization in parentheses. Plain, warm and concrete, no meta lines. Text in the wrong language is text the learner can't read.","id":"language","label":"Learner's language and tone"},{"expectations":"A person with no background in the subject follows it on the first read: everyday words, short sentences, and examples from everyday life they've seen or done, with a technical term only after the plain idea it names. Ask whether a grandmother would understand it. An output that is accurate but reads academic, abstract or dull fails its reader as surely as a wrong fact does: they stop reading or don't take it in. Notice jargon left unexplained, definitions before any example, abstract nouns where a concrete case fits, generic examples (\"a company\", \"a person\"), and a tone that reads like a textbook. A term the reader already knows for their goal is fine.","id":"plainLanguage","label":"Easy to follow"}]